n  imports of gas to get our power  right so are we keeping up though with  demand do we need much more  um and I'm saying that because just  yesterday we reported on the climate  show that oil and gas licenses are being  handed out by the government and of  course that's not great for the  environment a campaigners say so how  much more do we need  well the UK's current Target is to  Triple or actually just over triple our  current offshore wind capacity and  that's similar to what the the whole of  the EU is aiming for so we do have  strong Targets in this area but of  course as we know targets don't always  mean delivery and the government at the  moment is dragging its feet somewhat on  onshore wind which is actually even  cheaper than offshore wind so if it  starts to get its ducks in a row and  offshore wind help help people build  those wind turbines that we see offshore  and help people build them onshore as  well we can definitely make the most of  this opportunity we're such a windy  Nation you know it seems silly enough to  that's a very good point there and as we  were just saying even on settled days  we're doing pretty well when we were  looking at our data dashboard so where  is Britain as far as a global Renewables  are concerned are we doing pretty well  absolutely so we had early subsidies  back in the 2000s in the 2010s which  really helped to build our capacity in  offshore wind and helped grow those  Supply chains from a smaller startup  type industry to now a global superpower  when it comes to Offshore wind and this  is very much a global Trend so the US  and China and they're investing hundreds  of billions that's billions not Millions  into offshore wind and wind power in  general and that's because they see it  as the next Big Industry they're seeing  it as a growth area and the UK's got a  strong record but we do have a risk of  falling behind if we continue to drag  our heels on some policy and some  decision making particularly when it  